      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To determine the number of odd numbers between 100 and 999 with distinct digits, we need to consider the following constraints:\n1. The number must be odd, meaning the last digit must be one of {1, 3, 5, 7, 9}.\n2. The digits must be distinct.\n\nWe can break down the problem into cases based on the last digit being one of the possible odd digits.\n\n### Case 1: The last digit is 1\nThe first digit can be any digit from 1 to 9 (since the number is between 100 and 999), excluding the digit that will be the last digit. The second digit can be any digit from 0 to 9 that is not the same as the first or the last digit.\n\n- If the first digit is 1, the possible last digit choices are {3, 5, 7, 9} (4 choices).\n- The second digit can be any of the remaining 8 digits (10 options minus the first and last digits).\n\nThe total number of combinations for this case is:\n\\[ 9 \\times 8 \\times 4 = 288 \\]\n\n### Case 2: The last digit is 3\nSimilarly, the first digit can be any digit from 1 to 9, excluding the digit that will be the last digit. The second digit can be any digit from 0 to 9 that is not the same as the first or the last digit.\n\n- If the first digit is 3, the possible last digit choices are {1, 5, 7, 9} (4 choices).\n- The second digit can be any of the remaining 8 digits (10 options minus the first and last digits).\n\nThe total number of combinations for this case is:\n\\[ 9 \\times 8 \\times 4 = 288 \\]\n\n### Case 3: The last digit is 5\nSimilarly, the first digit can be any digit from 1 to 9, excluding the digit that will be the last digit. The second digit can be any digit from 0 to 9 that is not the same as the first or the last digit.\n\n- If the first digit is 5, the possible last digit choices are {1, 3, 7, 9} (4 choices).\n- The second digit can be any of the remaining 8 digits (10 options minus the first and last digits).\n\nThe total number of combinations for this case is:\n\\[ 9 \\times 8 \\times 4 = 288 \\]\n\n### Case 4: The last digit is 7\nSimilarly, the first digit can be any digit from 1 to 9, excluding the digit that will be the last digit. The second digit can be any digit from 0 to 9 that is not the same as the first or the last digit.\n\n- If the first digit is 7, the possible last digit choices are {1, 3, 5, 9} (4 choices).\n- The second digit can be any of the remaining 8 digits (10 options minus the first and last digits).\n\nThe total number of combinations for this case is:\n\\[ 9 \\times 8 \\times 4 = 288 \\]\n\n### Case 5: The last digit is 9\nSimilarly, the first digit can be any digit from 1 to 9, excluding the digit that will be the last digit. The second digit can be any digit from 0 to 9 that is not the same as the first or the last digit.\n\n- If the first digit is 9, the possible last digit choices are {1, 3, 5, 7} (4 choices).\n- The second digit can be any of the remaining 8 digits (10 options minus the first and last digits).\n\nThe total number of combinations for this case is:\n\\[ 9 \\times 8 \\times 4 = 288 \\]\n\n### Total number of odd numbers with distinct digits\nSumming up all the cases, we get:\n\\[ 288 + 288 + 288 + 288 + 288 = 1152 \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of odd numbers between 100 and 999 with distinct digits is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1152}\n\\]",
