    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the number of odd numbers between $100$ and $999$ with distinct digits, we first need to visualize the structure of the numbers we are looking for. \n\n1. **Identify the structure of the numbers:**\n   - A number between $100$ and $999$ has three digits: the hundreds, tens, and units place.\n   - The number must be odd, so the units digit must be one of $\\{1, 3, 5, 7, 9\\}$ (i.e., the digit that makes the number odd).\n\n2. **Choose the units digit:**\n   - There are $5$ possible choices for the units digit (1, 3, 5, 7, 9).\n\n3. **Choose the hundreds and tens digits:**\n   - After choosing the units digit, we have 9 remaining digits to choose from (since the digits must be distinct and cannot be the same as the units digit).\n   - For the hundreds digit, we have $9$ choices (any digit from 1 to 9, excluding the thousands digit which is $1$).\n   - For the tens digit, we have $8$ choices (any digit from 0 to 9, excluding the hundreds digit and the units digit).\n\n4. **Calculate the total number of such numbers:**\n   - We multiply the number of choices for each digit together:\n     \\[\n     9 \\text{ (choices for hundreds digit)} \\times 8 \\text{ (choices for tens digit)} \\times 5 \\text{ (choices for units digit)} = 360\n     \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of odd numbers between $100$ and $999$ with distinct digits is \\(\\boxed{360}\\).",
